Working on 9share

Layout
  src/              9share: main.c (the commands' names), cmds.c (export, mount, status ...), serve.c (the exporter), boot.c (the root
                    daemon and the mounts), util.c (files, paths, running programs, editing a config file's text), conf.c (the checks)
  src/relay.c       the receiving end of a link (`9share relay`, run by the daemon as the mount's user; `9share link`, run by ssh)
  src/9share_conf.c   the parser of 9share.conf: GENERATED, see below; src/hbnf_conf.{c,h} are its runtime support
  grammars/         9share.hbnf, the grammar of 9share.conf
  daud/src/liblink/ the resumable link (link.c), SCRAM-SHA-256 (auth.c) and its hash code (crypto.c); used by daud and by 9share
  tools/            9share-init (finds the init system), 9share-setup (the questions), regen-conf.sh
  etc/              the template of /etc/9share/9share.conf
  daud/             daud, the 9P server: diod v1.1.0 (git subtree of upstream, history kept) with our changes
  tests/            C tests and shell tests, run by `make check`
  man/, systemd/, debian/ (the init script, postinst and prerm too)

The parser of 9share.conf
  src/9share_conf.c is made by hbnf, the parser generator meta compiler (https://github.com/moebiusV/hbnf), from grammars/9share.hbnf.
  It is committed, and the build never needs hbnf.  To change what 9share.conf can say:
    1. edit grammars/9share.hbnf (a keyword, its argument, and the action that fills struct nineshare_conf, declared in src/9share_conf.h);
    2. add the check to src/conf.c if a value needs one, and a test to tests/test_conf.c;
    3. build hbnf (git clone https://github.com/moebiusV/hbnf; gprbuild -P hbnf.gpr; on a system without the static Ada runtime
       link it dynamically by removing "-static" from a copy of hbnf.gpr) and run
           HBNF=/path/to/hbnf/hbnf HBNF_TEMPLATES=/path/to/hbnf/templates tools/regen-conf.sh
    4. commit the grammar and the regenerated file together.
  src/hbnf_conf.c and hbnf_conf.h are copied unchanged from hbnf's runtime/; copy them again when you regenerate with a newer hbnf.

daud
  The changes to upstream diod are few (see `git log -- daud`): the program is built as daud 0.1.0 and nothing else is built; an
  attach that names no numeric uid is the running user's when there is no user database (libnpfs/user.c, so an unprivileged server
  needs no relay); the const parameter that GCC 15 rejects in liblsd/hostlist.c.  To take a newer diod:
      git subtree pull --prefix=daud https://github.com/chaos/diod.git TAG
  configure builds it with --disable-auth --disable-config --disable-multiuser (munge, lua and multi-user mode are not used).

Nothing in the repository may name a machine, a local user or a local path: tests/test_clean.sh looks for the ones of the machine
it runs on.  Examples use alice, bob, example.org and /srv/projects.

Tests
  `make -C tests check` needs no root.  Do not run the top-level `make check` casually: it also runs daud's own suite, which is slow.
  tests/test_serve.sh finds its processes with `pgrep -f`, which matches the whole command line of any process: a command you run
  the suite from that itself contains the text `--dial .* 9share link` or `sleep 600` (an editing script, say) makes its last check fail.
